# Exam Practice

## 1. Section Overview

This section helps students move from **learning content** to **answering exam-style questions**.

The main purpose is not only to practise questions, but also to learn how to answer them in the way mark schemes reward.

Students should learn how to:

```text
read command terms carefully
identify what the question is asking
select the correct topic knowledge
write answers using mark scheme keywords
link answers to the given scenario
show enough detail for the marks available
avoid vague answers
structure longer answers clearly
check pseudocode, flowcharts, and trace tables carefully
```

Exam practice is divided into separate pages so students can focus on one skill or topic group at a time.

---

## 2. Learning Goals

By the end of this Exam Practice section, students should be able to:

- explain the structure and purpose of Paper 1
- identify common command terms
- understand how mark schemes award marks
- distinguish short-answer, explain, compare, discuss, and algorithm-style questions
- write answers using mark scheme wording
- avoid common weak answer patterns
- answer B1 computational thinking questions
- answer B2 and B3 programming/OOP questions
- answer A1/A2 hardware, systems, and networks questions
- answer A3/A4 database and machine learning questions
- complete mini mock exam sets
- self-mark using mark scheme criteria
- improve answers after checking the mark scheme
- revise by topic and by skill

